// Reactivity S4 — a `set` performed *inside* a running effect coalesces into the ongoing flush rather
// than starting a nested one. A `copier` effect reads `src` and writes `mirror`; a `watch` effect reads
// `mirror`. The distinguishing evidence is ordering: because the copier's `mirror.set` only *enqueues*
// the watcher (the flush picks it up after the copier finishes), "copier-after" prints BEFORE the
// watcher's line — not between the copier's two prints, which is where a nested flush would have run it.
// Both backends share the one flush loop, so this order is byte-identical under the differential.
// expect: stdout "copier-before src=0"
// expect: stdout "copier-after"
// expect: stdout "watch mirror=0"
// expect: stdout "copier-before src=5"
// expect: stdout "copier-after"
// expect: stdout "watch mirror=5"
// expect: exit 0
src = signal(0)
mirror = signal(0)
effect(fn() {
    echo "copier-before src=${src.get()}";
    mirror.set(src.get());
    echo "copier-after"
})
effect(fn() {
    echo "watch mirror=${mirror.get()}"
})
src.set(5)
